@startuml AgentSettingsChoiceCache
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: how a harness run's capability choices are prompted, remembered and invalidated
' covers: internal/agentsettings/**
' covers: internal/choiceui/**
' covers: internal/wsscan/**
' covers: e2e/agent_settings_test.go
'
' ── WHAT AN E2E RUN HAS TO DO TO OBSERVE ANY OF THIS ────────────────────────
' The form is the cache's only door in BOTH directions — it is where a remembered
' answer is shown and where the answer to remember is given — so a run with the
' wizard off has nothing here to assert — and, for the same reason, takes the
' MANIFEST defaults rather than the operator's remembered posture. The harness e2e
' suites disable it by default for exactly that reason; the suite that is ABOUT the
' form turns it back on (`PROVEO_WIZARD=on`, which wins as a later NAME=value
' operand to env(1)). An empty workspace keeps the sub-project picker away, leaving
' the choice form as the only prompt on the PTY.
'
' Seeding agent-settings.yml is NOT a way to skip the prompt. The prompt still
' appears — that is the whole rule above — so any test that starts a run on a TTY
' has to answer it, and the accept is anchored on the key hint ("enter accept")
' rather than the form's title, which is copy and has been reworded once.
'
' ── THE EVIDENCE THE E2E LANE RECORDS ───────────────────────────────────────
' TestAgentSettingsPersistAcrossRuns drives two runs of opencode against one
' isolated HOME and reads the result from agent-settings.yml:
'   * the FIRST run must persist `egress: allowlist` and `credentials: broker` —
'     opencode's manifest defaults — and a NON-EMPTY fingerprint. A persisted choice
'     with no fingerprint is the failure mode where a manifest change could never
'     invalidate it.
'   * the file is then hand-edited to `egress: open`, and the SECOND run must
'     RE-ENTER that value and must NOT overwrite it. A machine default overwriting a
'     human's remembered answer is the cache acting as an authority in the opposite
'     direction.
' The tier is read from the AGENT's own environment — the session echoes
' `PROVEO_EGRESS_MODE` inside the container and only a line that is exactly the
' expansion counts, since the shell echoes the command line too. That is the honest
' signal that the choice took effect, rather than anything proveo printed host-side.
'
' Isolating HOME is what makes any of this deterministic: a shared HOME would leak
' into the developer's real settings AND skip the prompt on every later run. It also
' hides ~/.docker and with it the active context, so DOCKER_HOST has to be pinned
' explicitly or every docker call falls back to /var/run/docker.sock and hangs.

title Choice cache — the prompt always appears; the cache only seeds it

component "manifest ""capabilities""\negress · credentials · providers" as CAP <<app>>
component "choiceui form\none prompt, N rows" as TUI <<app>>
component "resolver\nflag > remembered > manifest default" as RES <<app>>
component "Fingerprint(capabilities)\nsha256, normalised, first 8 bytes" as FP <<app>>
component "Store.Lookup / Remember" as STORE <<app>>
database "``agent-settings.yml``\n""targets.<t>"" → egress · credentials ·\naddons · authVar · evidence · models · fingerprint" as FILE <<db>>
component "headless run\nno TTY" as HEADLESS <<host>>

CAP ARROW_MAIN TUI : the choice space
CAP ARROW_MAIN FP
FP ARROW_MAIN STORE
STORE ARROW_MAIN FILE
STORE ARROW_OPTIONAL RES : remembered answer
RES ARROW_MAIN TUI : seeds the selection
TUI ARROW_MAIN STORE : Remember
HEADLESS ARROW_QUEUE RES : manifest default, logged

note left of TUI
  **The form is the only door, both ways.** With the
  wizard off there is no remembered answer shown and
  none taken, so the run uses manifest defaults —
  an e2e lane asserting the cache must turn the
  wizard back ON before there is anything to see.
end note

note left of FILE
  **Read the tier from the AGENT's own env.** The
  e2e lane echoes ""PROVEO_EGRESS_MODE"" inside the
  container: what proveo printed host-side is an
  intention, and the container's env is the fact.
end note

note right of TUI
  **The matrix is data, not code.** A harness's constraint is declared in its
  manifest, not special-cased in ""cmd/proveo"" — which is what makes the choice
  space inspectable per def instead of hidden behind ""if target == …"".

  **A single-option axis is not a choice, so its row is dropped.** Rows appear
  only where something is actually decidable; the header shows ""\.env"" as
  loaded, **names only** for secrets (manifest ""env[]\.secret"" ∪
  ""provider\.KeyVars()"") and ""key=value"" for the rest.
end note

note right of RES
  **The prompt always appears; a remembered answer seeds it, never replaces
  it.** Silently entering a cached tier hid the security posture of the run.
  The operator must see — and be able to change — what they are launching, so
  the cache is an ergonomic shortcut and never an authority.
end note

note bottom of FP
  **Cached choices do not survive a manifest change.** The entry stores a
  fingerprint of the def's ""capabilities"" block, normalised (lower-cased,
  trimmed, sorted) so cosmetic reordering does not invalidate it. Any real
  change discards the entry and re-prompts, rather than silently keeping a tier
  the def no longer allows or dropping an add-on that no longer exists.
end note

note bottom of FILE
  **Models are remembered too, and are not a form row.** ""targets.<t>.models""
  records the last used ""main"" / ""editor"" / ""small"" as **canonical** ids, so a
  session mixing providers is reproducible without retyping three env vars.
  Canonical rather than harness-specific: a translated spelling would pin the
  entry to whatever the catalog said the day it was written, and a synced
  correction would never reach it.
end note

note bottom of CAP
  **""providers"" also closes a live failure.** In a brokered tier
  ""provider\.KeyVars()"" sentinel-injects every detected key, so the broker sees
  two or more, refuses to pin, and the agent receives the ""proveo-brokered""
  sentinel — which the provider answers with 403. Filtering detection through
  the def's declared providers keeps a stray key for a harness that cannot use
  it out of the set entirely.
end note

note bottom of HEADLESS
  E2E, CI and cron must never block on a human: with no TTY the resolver takes
  the manifest default and logs the choice it made, so a non-interactive run is
  still auditable after the fact.

  **The code read the cache here anyway, for one release cycle.** ""Lookup"" was
  applied unconditionally and ""Remember"" wrote on every non-dry run, so the rule
  above held only for the //prompt//, never for the resolution. The consequence
  was not cosmetic: a headless run's **security posture** came from whatever the
  last interactive session happened to pick. An e2e run asking for the default
  ""--credentials broker"" silently got ""forward"", and with it a ""browser"" image
  variant it never selected — and then rewrote the operator's remembered posture
  on its way out.

  Both halves now hang off the same predicate (""cacheApplies""): where there is
  no prompt to seed, the cache is neither read nor written. Writing mattered as
  much as reading — a machine default overwriting a human's remembered answer is
  the cache acting as an authority in the opposite direction.
end note

@enduml
